  65: <h1>Hospital distress �?" the full census</h1>
  66: <p class="sub">Every Medicare-participating hospital's annual cost report, FY2016�?"FY2025
  67: A� 5,747 hospitals in the latest complete year A� generated 2026-11-16 12:32 UTC</p>
  68: 
  69: <div class="answer">
  70: <h3>Are more hospitals distressed than in the past?</h3>
  71: <p><strong>No �?" fewer hospitals are losing money than before the pandemic.</strong>
  72: 28.7% of hospitals ran a negative total margin in FY2025, against
  73: 32.0% in FY2016 and a pre-pandemic norm around 32�?"35%.</p>
  74: <p>The real event is <strong>FY2022</strong>, when 41.3% lost money
  75: as pandemic relief ran out and labour costs stayed high �?" and it hit
  76: <strong>nonprofits hardest</strong>, 47% of
  77: them in the red with a median margin of
  78: +0.6%. The sector has recovered every year
  79: since. That is the opposite of a slow bleed, and it matters for reading the bond screen:
  80: distressed hospital credits there are <em>individual</em> situations, not a sector in
  81: decline.</p>
  82: <p>Government hospitals are the persistent exception �?" a median margin near
  83: +2.9% even in a good year, structurally
  84: thinner than either nonprofits or for-profits throughout.</p>
  85: </div>
  86: 
  87: <h2>Share of hospitals losing money, by ownership</h2>
