 113:     <h2>Fiscal-period reconciliation</h2>
 114:     <div class="legend">
 115:       <p><b>127 of 127</b> measured cells are calendar-aligned; <b>0</b> are not. Period basis across live rows �?"
 116:       <b>13</b> measured (the filing states start and end dates),
 117:       <b>4</b> point-in-time snapshots at a month end, and
 118:       <b>32</b> assumed calendar (marked <span class="basis">~</span>:
 119:       <code>earnings_kpi</code> stores a fiscal quarter label with no dates, and managed-care
 120:       filers are calendar filers, but this sheet says it is assuming that rather than implying
 121:       it checked). <b>On those rows the period-length comparability check could not run
 122:       either</b> �?" a 53rd week or a ragged quarter passes through as growth there, where a
 123:       dated row would have been withheld. That is the cost of the missing dates, and it is
 124:       larger than the placement question it is usually described as.</p>
 125:       <p>Every cell is placed by the <b>nearest calendar quarter end</b> to its reporting
 126:       period's end, so a fiscal quarter running Nov&ndash;Jan lands in calendar Q4, not Q1.
 127:       (This paragraph said "mid-point" until 2026-08-08; the code has always snapped on the
 128:       end. The two agree for a 13-week quarter, which is why it went unnoticed �?" but a sheet
 129:       whose whole claim is honesty about method cannot misdescribe its own.)
 130:       Fiscal year-end offset is not
 131:       the same thing as period-boundary offset: McKesson (March year-end), Cencora (September)
 132:       and Cardinal Health (June) all carry fiscal <i>labels</i> that differ from the calendar
 133:       while their period <i>dates</i> line up exactly.</p>
